  RHAISTRAT-9012-E001 --> RHAISTRAT-9012-E002\n    RHAISTRAT-9012-E001 --> RHAISTRAT-9012-E003\n    RHAISTRAT-9012-E001 --> RHAISTRAT-9012-E005\n    RHAISTRAT-9012-E002 --> RHAISTRAT-9012-E004\n    RHAISTRAT-9012-E003 --> RHAISTRAT-9012-E005\n    RHAISTRAT-9012-E005 --> RHAISTRAT-9012-E006\n    RHAISTRAT-9012-E005 --> RHAISTRAT-9012-E007\n    RHAISTRAT-9012-E002 --> RHAISTRAT-9012-E008\n    RHAISTRAT-9012-E003 --> RHAISTRAT-9012-E008\n    RHAISTRAT-9012-E004 --> RHAISTRAT-9012-E008\n    RHAISTRAT-9012-E005 --> RHAISTRAT-9012-E008\n    RHAISTRAT-9012-E006 --> RHAISTRAT-9012-E008\n    RHAISTRAT-9012-E007 --> RHAISTRAT-9012-E008\n```\n\n## DAG Justification\n\n| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n|------|------|-----------|\n| E001 → E002 | Rule 3 (Investigation gate) | The legal clearance outcome selects the upstream (MinIO vs. Apache-2.0 fallback); repo onboarding is a true gate with `rewrite` impact. |\n| E001 → E003 | Rule 3 (Investigation gate) | The dependency tree to be scanned depends on which upstream is selected; `rewrite` on fallback. |\n| E001 → E005 | Rule 3 (Investigation gate) | The operator integration scope (which store is bundled) is directly rewritten if the fallback upstream is selected. |\n| E002 → E004 | Rule 5 (repo-onboarding → konflux-onboarding serial) | The Konflux build pipeline needs the midstream repository to exist. |\n| E003 → E005 | Rule 9 (license-validation → general implementation serial) | If license validation fails, the dependency set changes and the approach changes. |\n| (no edge) E004 ↔ E005 | Rule 10 (konflux-onboarding ∥ general implementation) | Konflux onboarding and the operator implementation run in parallel; coupling is enforced by the \"build pipeline green\" and \"pinned image digest\" acceptance criteria on the implementation epic, never by a DAG edge. |\n| (no edge) E002 → E005 | Rule 6 / Rule 13 note | The operator work lives in the Platform Operator repo (repo-onboarding does not block other repos); the productized image digest is a configuration reference filled in at execution time — an AC gate, not a DAG edge. |\n| E005 → E006 | Rule 13 (build-against artifact) | The operator epic defines how the bundled store is exposed (endpoint/credential discovery); the pipelines defaults build against that contract. |\n| E005 → E007 | Rule 13 (build-against artifact) | Same exposure contract consumed by the model serving defaults. |\n| E002, E003, E004, E005, E006, E007 → E008 | Rule 11 (docs last) | Documentation is blocked by every Implementation epic; it describes what was built. These edges do not trigger priority inheritance (docs-authoring exception). |\n\n## HLR Traceability Matrix\n\n| HLR | Priority | Epics |\n|-----|----------|-------|\n| HLR-1 | P0 | E001 |\n| HLR-2 | P0 | E002, E003, E004, E005 |\n| HLR-3 | P1 | E006, E007 |\n| HLR-4 | P2 | E008 |\n\nFull coverage: every HLR maps to at least one epic; no orphaned requirements.\n\n## Investigation Outcome Modeling\n\nThe legal clearance has two bounded outcomes: (A) AGPL-3.0 redistribution of\nMinIO is cleared; (B) terms cannot be satisfied and the Apache-2.0 fallback\n(e.g. SeaweedFS) is selected. Both outcomes yield the same epic structure —\nthe same onboarding chain, operator integration, connection defaults, and\ndocumentation — with the productization epics' scope rewritten to the\nselected upstream. The outcome is therefore modeled as `gated_by` +\n`gate_failure_impact: rewrite` gates on the directly affected epics (E002,\nE003, E005) rather than conditional branch epics: no epic exists in one\noutcome but not the other, and downstream epics (connection defaults,\ndocumentation) are upstream-agnostic.\n\n## Component Mapping Notes\n\n- The bundled object store (MinIO / SeaweedFS) is a new component with no\n  canonical Jira component name in `.context/rhai-components.txt` and no\n  architecture context file. Per Rule 20 it gets the full onboarding chain\n  (repo-onboarding + license-validation in parallel → konflux-onboarding →\n  general implementation). Its productization epics (E002–E004) are filed\n  under the canonical component `Platform Operator`, the strategy component\n  they most directly serve.\n- Model serving (HLR-3) is mapped to the canonical component `KServe` (see\n  health warning below).\n\n## Health Warnings\n\n- **Missing strategy sections**: no Impacted Teams table, no Dependencies\n  table, no Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, and no non-functional\n  requirements. Team assignments are inferred from the platform architecture\n  context (RHOAI Dashboard / RHOAI Pipelines are documented owners) and\n  platform naming conventions for the remainder (RHOAI Platform Operator,\n  RHOAI Serving, RHOAI Documentation) — confirm with stakeholders.\n- **Model serving not in Affected Components**: HLR-3 and the problem\n  statement require model serving connection defaults, but the Affected\n  Components list omits any model serving component. An epic was created\n  under the canonical component `KServe`; the strategy's component list\n  should be corrected.\n- **Dashboard is passive**: Dashboard is listed in Affected Components, but\n  no HLR requires Dashboard changes (the Dashboard reads CRs and owns no data\n  plane). Handled as a passive component: a validation acceptance criterion\n  on the Data Science Pipelines defaults epic.\n- **HLR-4 priority vs docs rule**: HLR-4 is labeled P2, but per the\n  docs-authoring priority exception the documentation epic derives P1 from\n  the strategy frontmatter priority (Major → P1).\n- **Schedule risk (not an AC)**: legal review turnaround is externally\n  controlled and cannot be compressed; by explicit HLR-1 statement it blocks\n  all shipping work. Recorded here rather than as an acceptance criterion.\n\n## Tiered Delivery\n\nEvaluated per Step 5.5 for the legal-clearance dependency with uncertain\ntiming: the strategy states the clearance \"blocks all shipping work,\" and no\nimplementation epic delivers user-visible value without a productized store.\nTier 1 is therefore the risk-reduction work only (the legal investigation\nitself); all productization, integration, and defaults work is Tier 2,\ndependency-gated. No partial-value split is viable.\n"}}],"model":"rits/zai-org/glm-5-3","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":48681,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-90dccf0358d8875d","agent_id":"aaf52d2e558d179d4","session_id":"aee89738-3ddf-45ac-bec8-585fb72c96b4","uuid":"6d871b3d-be16-4641-88fd-014f17fd72b2","timestamp":"2026-10-22T16:57:13.272Z","subagent_type":"general-purpose","task_description":"Decompose RHAISTRAT-9012 into epics"}
